  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "The Sifting Property",
    "lesson_core_move": "Apply the sifting property to convert an integral of a continuous-time signal multiplied by a shifted unit impulse into the signal value sampled at the impulse location.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "The Sifting Property",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to The Sifting Property",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When you see $x(\\tau)\\,\\delta(\\tau-t_0)$ inside an integral, read the shifted impulse as a sampling cue. It marks the one time that survives, $\\tau=t_0$, and tells you to keep the value of $x(\\tau)$ there.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The clean base case is the impulse at the origin. If the center is $\\tau=0$, the integral returns the signal value at $0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(\\tau)\\,\\delta(\\tau)\\,d\\tau = x(0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The shifted version keeps the same rule. Moving the impulse center from $0$ to $t_0$ moves the sampled value with it.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(\\tau)\\,\\delta(\\tau-t_0)\\,d\\tau = x(t_0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the sifting property is a substitution pattern, not a rule for integrating $x(\\tau)$ term by term. Identify the impulse location, substitute that time into $x(\\tau)$, and write the sampled result directly as $x(t_0)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "The Sifting Property",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Sifting a Signal with an Impulse at the Origin",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} (\\tau^2+4\\tau+1)\\,\\delta(\\tau)\\,d\\tau$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the origin-centered impulse $\\delta(\\tau)$, so this is a sampling problem at $\\tau=0$. By the sifting property, the impulse keeps only the signal value at the origin.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} (\\tau^2+4\\tau+1)\\,\\delta(\\tau)\\,d\\tau = x(0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here $x(\\tau)=\\tau^2+4\\tau+1$, so the sample at the origin is found by substituting $\\tau=0$ into the signal expression.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(0)=0^2+4(0)+1=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the integral evaluates to $1$. The impulse does not make you integrate the polynomial term by term; it makes you read off the sampled value at the impulse location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "The Sifting Property",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Sifting a Signal with a Shifted Impulse",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} (\\cos\\tau+2)\\,\\delta(\\tau-1.5)\\,d\\tau$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the shifted impulse $\\delta(\\tau-1.5)$, so the sample point is $t_0=1.5$. By the sifting property, the integral keeps only the value of the signal at that time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} (\\cos\\tau+2)\\,\\delta(\\tau-1.5)\\,d\\tau = x(1.5)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here $x(\\tau)=\\cos\\tau+2$, so substitute $\\tau=1.5$ into the signal expression.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(1.5)=\\cos(1.5)+2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the integral evaluates to $\\cos(1.5)+2$. The shift in the impulse simply moves the sampling location from $0$ to $1.5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "The Sifting Property",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Sifting a Time-Domain Formula at the Impulse Location",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} (\\tau^3+\\tau-\\frac{1}{\\tau})\\,\\delta(\\tau+1)\\,d\\tau$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the shifted impulse $\\delta(\\tau+1)$, which is the same as $\\delta(\\tau-(-1))$. So the sample point is $t_0=-1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} (\\tau^3+\\tau-\\frac{1}{\\tau})\\,\\delta(\\tau+1)\\,d\\tau = x(-1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here $x(\\tau)=\\tau^3+\\tau-\\frac{1}{\\tau}$, so substitute $\\tau=-1$ into the signal expression.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}x(-1) &= (-1)^3+(-1)-\\frac{1}{-1} \\\\ &= -1-1+1 \\\\ &= -1\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the integral evaluates to $-1$. The shifted impulse simply moves the sampling location from $0$ to $-1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
